>> title: Mango Chutney
>> category: Dressings & Dips
>> tags: indian, mango, preserve, sweet, condiment
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: major grey chutney, sweet mango chutney, aam ki chutney, mango preserve
>> pairs-with: papadom, raita
>> servings: 3 cups
>> time: 2 hr 15 min
>> step.1: salt and stand 1 hr, then tip off the water
>> step.2: fry 1 min, until the seeds pop
>> step.3: simmer 5 min to a syrup
>> step.4: simmer 45 min, until it falls thickly off the spoon

Rest @green mangoes{2%lb}(900 g; hard and unripe, peeled and cut in 1/2-in dice) under @kosher salt{1%Tbs} ~stand{1%hr}, then pour off the water they give up.

Fry @brown mustard seeds{2%tsp}, @nigella seeds{1%tsp}, @fresh ginger{2%Tbs}(cut in matchsticks), @garlic{4%cloves}(sliced) and @dried red chiles{3}(broken) in @neutral oil{2%Tbs}(30 mL) in a #heavy pot{} ~fry{1%min}, until the mustard seeds jump.

Simmer @granulated sugar{1 1/2%cup}(300 g) and @cider vinegar{1%cup}(240 mL) with @&(~1)tempering{} ~simmer{5%min}, stirring until the sugar has gone.

Simmer @&(~3)salted mango{} in @&(~1)syrup{} ~simmer{45%min}, uncovered and stirred now and then, until the fruit is translucent and a spoonful dropped on a cold plate holds its edge.

Unripe fruit is the point: a ripe mango goes to mush in twenty minutes and gives you jam. This is the sweet corner of the chutney tray and the one that goes on the papadom first. It keeps six months in sterilised jars and a month open in the fridge, and it is better after a fortnight than on the day.
